WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.620
How can you watch single cells work deep inside living tissue?

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.100
Wiring maps show which cells touch, yet cannot show living cells firing.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.480
Two-photon microscopy lights only one deep focus.

4
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.660
The microscope aims an infrared laser beam into living tissue.

5
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.720
Paired infrared photons excite fluorescence only at the focus.

6
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.780
Fluorescence needs two photons arriving together, which happens only where the beam

7
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.240
is tightest. Scanning that spot maps living cells at depth.

8
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:42.150
To see neurons fire, a genetically encoded sensor turns spikes into light.

9
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:49.270
After an action potential, voltage-gated calcium channels open and cell-body calcium

10
00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:56.230
rises. GCaMP6 joins a green fluorescent protein to calmodulin, a calcium-binding

11
00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:03.150
protein. Calcium-bound calmodulin grips peptide M13, changing shape to brighten the

12
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:03.690
green barrel. Calcium -bound calmodulin grips peptide M13, changing shape to

13
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:04.630
brighten the green barrel.

14
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:09.973
From that glow, we compare fluorescence now, F, with resting baseline, F0.

15
00:01:11.453 --> 00:01:16.033
Dividing the fluorescence change by baseline gives ΔF/F0.

16
00:01:17.273 --> 00:01:22.373
With background light removed, dividing cancels shared scales like laser power or

17
00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:29.093
sensor amount. Yet cells stay unequal: expression changes calcium buffering, and

18
00:01:29.093 --> 00:01:30.253
photon noise differs.

19
00:01:30.253 --> 00:01:31.253
F0.

20
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:35.817
That photon noise sets a sharp trade-off when you image wider regions.

21
00:01:37.377 --> 00:01:41.117
Suppose you enlarge the field of view while keeping the same imaging time.

22
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:44.957
What happens to the small glow from a single spike?

23
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.870
As Huang and colleagues explained, the answer is photons.

24
00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:00.790
A larger field spreads imaging time over more pixels, so each neuron returns fewer

25
00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:07.310
photons. Photon counts flicker randomly, and one spike's small glow sinks into that

26
00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:13.810
flicker. In a simple model, a slowly decaying signal survives slower framing, if

27
00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.070
photons hold steady.

28
00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.210
Frame rate still limits spike timing.

29
00:02:19.283 --> 00:02:22.703
Light is not spikes, so these traces need a dictionary.

30
00:02:24.223 --> 00:02:28.223
Experimenters record light and spikes together in a sample of neurons.

31
00:02:29.603 --> 00:02:34.863
A fine glass pipette pressed outside one cell picks up spikes electrically without

32
00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:40.623
breaking in. That cell-attached answer key gives an estimate with error, not an

33
00:02:40.623 --> 00:02:41.623
exact lookup.

34
00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:48.217
In 2005, Ohki, Reid and colleagues imaged single cells in visual cortex.

35
00:02:49.797 --> 00:02:54.977
Rat V1 showed orientation-tuned cells with no discernible local map, called

36
00:02:54.977 --> 00:03:01.797
salt-and-pepper. The method was not blind to maps: in cat area 18, direction-column

37
00:03:01.797 --> 00:03:03.737
borders were one or two cells wide.

38
00:03:04.957 --> 00:03:06.257
So tuning needs no map.

39
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:12.390
In 2016, Baden and colleagues imaged the retinal ganglion cell layer.

40
00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.330
Grouping cells by light-response profile gave at least 32 functional types.

41
00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.790
The method also films microglia, the brain's resident immune cells.

42
00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:31.250
In healthy adult mouse neocortex, Nimmerjahn, Kirchhoff and Helmchen saw presumed

43
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:35.050
resting microglia continually extend and retract processes.

44
00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:41.590
Most such imaging used anesthetized mice; in awake mice, Stowell and colleagues

45
00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:42.750
found less surveillance.

46
00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.970
Lighting one deep focus lets us watch intact living cells work.
